{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Support Vector Machines Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 1: Hinge loss and gradient"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "The foundation. Compute hinge loss for a batch and its gradient."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def hinge_loss(X, y, w, b):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    n = len(X)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    total_loss = 0.0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for i in range(n):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        margin = y[i] * (dot(w, X[i]) + b)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        total_loss += max(0.0, 1.0 - margin)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return total_loss / n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 2: Linear SVM via gradient descent"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Train by minimizing regularized hinge loss. No QP solver needed."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "class LinearSVM:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    def __init__(self, lr=0.001, lambda_param=0.01, n_epochs=1000):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.lr = lr"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.lambda_param = lambda_param"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.n_epochs = n_epochs"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.w = None"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.b = 0.0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    def fit(self, X, y):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        n_features = len(X[0])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.w = [0.0] * n_features"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        self.b = 0.0"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        for epoch in range(self.n_epochs):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            for i in range(len(X)):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                margin = y[i] * (dot(self.w, X[i]) + self.b)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                if margin >= 1:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                    self.w = [wj - self.lr * self.lambda_param * wj"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                              for wj in self.w]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                else:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                    self.w = [wj - self.lr * (self.lambda_param * wj - y[i] * X[i][j])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                              for j, wj in enumerate(self.w)]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "                    self.b -= self.lr * (-y[i])"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    def predict(self, X):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        return [1 if dot(self.w, x) + self.b >= 0 else -1 for x in X]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 3: Kernel functions"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Implement linear, polynomial, and RBF kernels."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def linear_kernel(x, z):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return dot(x, z)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def polynomial_kernel(x, z, degree=3, c=1.0):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return (dot(x, z) + c) ** degree"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def rbf_kernel(x, z, gamma=0.5):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    diff = [xi - zi for xi, zi in zip(x, z)]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return math.exp(-gamma * dot(diff, diff))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 4: Margin and support vector identification"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "After training, identify which points are support vectors and compute the margin width."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def find_support_vectors(X, y, w, b, tol=1e-3):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    support_vectors = []"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for i in range(len(X)):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        margin = y[i] * (dot(w, X[i]) + b)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        if abs(margin - 1.0) < tol:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "            support_vectors.append(i)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return support_vectors"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "See `code/svm.py` for the complete implementation with all demos."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Generate a 2D linearly separable dataset. Train your LinearSVM and identify the support vectors. Verify that the support vectors are the points closest to the decision boundary.\n\n2. Vary C from 0.001 to 1000 on a noisy dataset. Plot the decision boundary for each C value. Observe the transition from wide margin (underfitting) to narrow margin (overfitting).\n\n3. Create a dataset where class boundaries are circular (not linear). Show that a linear SVM fails. Compute the RBF kernel matrix and show that the classes become separable in the kernel-induced feature space.\n\n4. Compare hinge loss vs logistic loss on the same dataset. Train a linear SVM and logistic regression. Count how many training points contribute to each model's decision boundary (support vectors vs all points).\n\n5. Implement SVR (epsilon-insensitive loss). Fit it to y = sin(x) + noise. Plot the epsilon tube around the predictions and highlight the support vectors (points outside the tube)."
      ]
    }
